Project Blueprint: DevBrand AI

"The Automated Proof-of-Work Engine for Developers"
I. Summary
DevBrand AI is a professional branding automation tool designed specifically for software engineers. It bridges the gap between a developer’s daily coding activity (GitHub) and their professional visibility (LinkedIn). By using a "Narrative Engine," the platform converts raw code commits into high-engagement social proof, helping developers stay "relevant" to recruiters without spending hours on social media.

II. The Problem Space

• Visibility Decay: A developer’s LinkedIn profile often becomes a "static resume" that doesn't reflect their current, evolving skill set.
• The Content Tax: Writing about code is a different skill than writing code. Most developers suffer from "blank page syndrome" when trying to post updates.
• Recruiter Psychology: Recruiters value consistency and "proof of work." A profile that shows weekly technical growth is significantly more likely to get a DM than a profile that hasn't been updated in six months.

III. Product Solution: The Narrative Engine

The core technology is a three-stage pipeline that ensures quality over quantity:
1. Ingestion: Daily/Weekly polling of GitHub Public Repositories.

2.The Impact Filter: An AI-driven classifier that ignores "noise" (typos, chores) and identifies "signal" (features, architecture, logic).

3.The "Ghostwriter" UI: Instead of auto-posting (which feels like spam), the app presents the user with curated "Daily Drafts." The user can edit, approve, or discard with one click.

IV. Technical Roadmap & Scalability

• Phase 1 (MVP): GitHub API integration + LinkedIn API integration. Focused on text-based posts from public repositories commit messages.
• Phase 2 (Diffs): The system can read git diff charts and create posts based on what the user has changed in a case where the commit messages aren’t “good” enough for a post (or just in general)
• Phase 3 (Visuals): Integration with README assets. The AI detects new screenshots in the images/ folder of a repo and attaches them to the social post. If images do not exist in the README, an AI image would be generated in it’s place??
• Phase 4 (Multipost): The app would be able to post to multiple social media accounts instead of just LinkedIn.
• Phase 5 (Enterprise/B2B): A "Team Mode" for engineering managers to showcase the "velocity" of their department on the company's LinkedIn page. (still under consideration)
• Scalability: The business scales through a Freemium model. Free users get 1 "Post-Worthiness" check a week; Pro users get daily analysis and "Voice Training" (teaching the AI to write exactly like them).

V. Competitive Landscape
While social media schedulers and AI writers exist, DevBrand AI is unique in its Deep Source Integration. By "reading" the code (the Diffs), the AI can provide a level of technical accuracy that a general-purpose AI (like ChatGPT) cannot achieve without manual input.

VI. Critical Success Factors
• Authenticity: The AI must avoid "AI-isms" (e.g., "In today's fast-paced tech landscape..."). It must sound like a human developer.
• Security: Users must trust the app with GitHub/LinkedIn tokens. SOC2 compliance or clear "Read-Only" permissions are vital.
• The "So What?" Factor: The engine must always link a technical change to a business result or a learning milestone.

